    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5397\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to authorize the village of Nichols, in the county of Tioga, to\ndiscontinue the use of certain lands as parklands\n\n\n PURPOSE :\nThis legislation is proposed in order to allow the Village of Nichols,\nTioga County, to discontinue as parkland a specific parcel of land\n(plus/minus 0.7 acres) located within the Village, as described in the\nbill.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 authorizes the Village of Nichols to convey park land for\nthe purposes drilling a well.\n\nSection 2 requires park land of equal or greater value to be added in\nexchange for the alienation.\n\nSections 3 and 4 details the land to be alienated.\n\nSection 5 details the land to be dedicated to park land purposes.\n\nSections 6 and 7 voids the transaction if the new parkland isn't of\nequal or greater value and requires the transaction to meet Federal\nrequirements.\n\nSection 8 is the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nThe Village of Nichols is under an obligation to maintain the\nintegrity of their municipal water supply. The private community water\nprovider in the Village of Nichols lost its production well located on\nJohnson Street in the Village of Nichols in June of 1994 due to ground\nwater contamination (nitrates). Since that time, the water supply has\ncome solely from a second well located on River Road in the Village.\nMany precautions have been taken to ensure the reliability of this\nwell; however a second source of supply is necessary to ensure the\nreliability of the system. In addition, NYS DEC requires\nmunicipal/community water systems to have redundant drinking water\nsources.\n\nAfter reviewing other possibilities and consulting with United Water,\nthe water provider, who tested many other sites in and around the\nvillage with unsuccessful results at each site except for Kirby Park,\nthe Village Board has identified a piece of village-owned parkland\n(approximately 0.7 acres) which they feel is best suited for a new\nwell. The Village Board has passed and presented a resolution to its\nstate legislators requesting authority to alienate this parkland. The\nbill would require the Village to dedicate new land of equal or\ngreater value than the property to be alienated as parkland. The\nVillage has identified this land, approximately 1 .16 acres, and it is\ndescribed in the bill.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\nNew bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nNone to State.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
