    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5397A\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to authorize the village of Nichols, in the\ncounty of Tioga, to discontinue the use of certain lands as parklands\n\n PURPOSE :  This legislation is proposed in order to allow the Village\nof Nichols, Tioga County, to discontinue as parkland a specific parcel\nof land (plus/minus 0.7 acres) located within the Village, as\ndescribed in the bill.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :  Section 1 authorizes the Village of Nichols\nto convey park land for the purposes drilling a well.\n\nSection 2 requires park land of equal or greater value to be added in\nexchange for the alienation.\n\nSections 3 and 4 details the land to be alienated.\n\nSection 5 details the land to be dedicated to park land purposes.\n\nSections 6 and 7 voids the transaction if the new parkland isn't of\nequal or greater value and requires the transaction to meet Federal\nrequirements.\n\nSection 8 is the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :  The Village of Nichols is under an obligation to\nmaintain the integrity of their municipal water supply. The private\ncommunity water provider in the Village of Nichols lost its production\nwell located on Johnson Street in the Village of Nichols in June of\n1994 due to ground water contamination (nitrates). Since that time,\nthe water supply has come solely from a second well located on River\nRoad in the Village. Many precautions have been taken to ensure the\nreliability of this well; however a second source of supply is\nnecessary to ensure the reliability of the system. In addition, NYS\nDEC requires municipal/community water systems to have redundant\ndrinking water sources. After reviewing other possibilities and\nconsulting with United Water, the water provider, who tested many\nother sites in and around the village with unsuccessful results at\neach site except for Kirby Park, the Village Board has identified a\npiece of village-owned parkland(approximately 0.7 acres) which they\nfeel is best suited for a new well.\n\nThe Village Board has passed and presented a resolution to its state\nlegislators requesting authority to alienate this parkland, The bill\nwould require the Village to dedicate new land of equal or greater\nvalue than the property to be alienated as parkland. The Village has\nidentified this land, approximately 1 in the bill.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY : New bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS : None to State.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE : Immediately.\n",
